#:blog{:title
       "A Wolf in Sheep's Clothing: Swappable Preludes and Hybrid Effects in Yin/Yang",
       :date #inst "2026-11-21T04:10:00.000-00:00",
       :abstract
       [:p
        "Most developers do not reject Clojure because of immutable data structures, functional pipelines, or data-oriented design. They bounce off parentheses. By decoupling Yang syntax frontends from their runtime preludes and splitting the runtime into a pure host value core, unified guest algebraic effects over %capture, and minimal VM stream effects, datom.world lets Python and TypeScript syntax compile directly onto clojure.core while keeping native preludes intact for existing code."],
       :content
       [:section.blog-article
        [:div.section-inner
         [:article
          :$blog-title
          :$blog-date

          [:p
           "Most developers who walk away from Clojure do not reject persistent data structures, immutability, or composing small functions over data. They bounce off parentheses. That is a syntax barrier, not a semantic one."]

          [:p
           "In datom.world, Axiom 2 states that "
           [:em "Interpretation Creates Semantics"]
           ". A Concrete Syntax Tree (CST) carries syntax only; its runtime meaning emerges from the lowering profile and runtime prelude paired with it. If syntax and semantics are truly separate, we can do two things at once: run existing Python, TypeScript, PHP, and Clojure programs under their own native preludes, and let developers write new datom.world programs in familiar Python or TypeScript syntax compiled directly onto "
           [:code "clojure.core"]
           ", persistent Yin values, and "
           [:code "dao.stream"]
           "."]

          [:p
           "In other words: Clojure as a wolf in sheep's clothing."]

          [:h2 "What Python syntax over clojure.core looks like"]

          [:p
           "Consider an ordinary Python function compiled under the "
           [:code ":yang.python/clojure-core-v1"]
           " semantics descriptor:"]

          [:pre
           [:code
            "def sum_even_squares(xs):\n"
            "    if is_empty(xs):\n"
            "        return 0\n"
            "    evens = filter(lambda n: n % 2 == 0, xs)\n"
            "    squares = map(lambda n: n * n, evens)\n"
            "    return reduce(lambda a, b: a + b, 0, squares)\n"]]

          [:p
           "Under the native "
           [:code "py"]
           " prelude, this lowers onto CPython-style mutable lists, numeric booleans, and tagged float records. Under "
           [:code ":yang.python/clojure-core-v1"]
           ", the exact same CST lowers onto persistent Yin vectors, maps, and sets, with "
           [:code "is_empty"]
           ", "
           [:code "filter"]
           ", "
           [:code "map"]
           ", and "
           [:code "reduce"]
           " projected directly from "
           [:code "clojure.core"]
           " through authenticated "
           [:code "yang.call"]
           " callables. Calling "
           [:code "sum_even_squares([1, 2, 3, 4])"]
           " evaluates to the exact persistent integer "
           [:code "20"]
           " with zero marshalling and zero mutable-object emulation overhead."]

          [:p
           "Honesty matters here. This profile does not pretend to be CPython: it explicitly declares Clojure truthiness (only "
           [:code "nil"]
           " and "
           [:code "false"]
           " are falsy, so "
           [:code "0"]
           " and "
           [:code "[]"]
           " are truthy), immutable collections, non-numeric booleans, and kind-strict equality ("
           [:code "1 != 1.0"]
           "). Existing Python code keeps running under the native "
           [:code "py"]
           " prelude; new datom.world scripts and agents can opt into Pythonic syntax directly over Yin's persistent data model."]

          [:h2 "The wall we hit: fighting host leaks in guest UAST"]

          [:p
           "Getting to this design cleanly was not straightforward. Our first five revisions of "
           [:code "docs/design/yang.preludes.md"]
           " went through four adversarial architect reviews and grew to 110 KB. Looking back at those forty findings, almost all of the churn came from one mistake: trying to patch host-language representation leaks inside guest Universal AST."]

          [:p
           "Yin's runtime values are host values across four hosts: JVM, Node/CLJS, Dart/CLJD, and Jolt (Clojure on Chez Scheme). Pure UAST control flow ("
           [:code "fn"]
           ", "
           [:code "if"]
           ", "
           [:code "let"]
           ", "
           [:code "%capture"]
           ") runs identically everywhere. Host differences leak specifically through unwrapped bare primitives:"]

          [:ul
           [:li
            [:strong "Bare arithmetic:"]
            " JVM "
            [:code "Long"]
            " overflows at 2^63 - 1, while Node/CLJS boxes floats in "
            [:code "Float64"]
            " and uses JS "
            [:code "BigInt"]
            " (which throws if mixed with JS "
            [:code "Number"]
            ")."]
           [:li
            [:strong "Bare equality:"]
            " On JVM, "
            [:code "(= ##NaN ##NaN)"]
            " is "
            [:code "false"]
            ", "
            [:code "(= 0.0 -0.0)"]
            " is "
            [:code "true"]
            ", and "
            [:code "(= (keyword nil \"a/b\") (keyword \"a\" \"b\"))"]
            " is "
            [:code "false"]
            ". On Node/CLJS, "
            [:code "Float64.-equiv"]
            " flips the "
            [:code "NaN"]
            " and signed-zero answers, and joined keyword storage collapses those two distinct keywords into one."]
           [:li
            [:strong "Host collections and formatting:"]
            " Different HAMT layouts make "
            [:code "keys"]
            ", "
            [:code "vals"]
            ", and "
            [:code "vec"]
            " iterate in different orders across JVM, Node, and Dart, while host "
            [:code "str"]
            " prints "
            [:code "1.0"]
            " as "
            [:code "\"1.0\""]
            " on JVM and "
            [:code "\"1\""]
            " on Node."]
           [:li
            [:strong "Raw-double stack frames:"]
            " Unwrapping a boxed float into a raw host double inside guest UAST forced fragile rules forbidding "
            [:code "%capture"]
            ", safepoints, or callbacks while any raw double was live on the stack."]]

          [:p
           "Patching around those leaks in guest UAST required recursive key validators ("
           [:code "admit-key!"]
           "), an O(n log n) guest-UAST "
           [:code "canonical-compare"]
           " sort, manual float string formatting, and four separate control-flow subsystems (exceptions, generators, safepoints, and status-keyword limit checks). When every review round adds new defensive guard rails on top of old ones, the problem is not missing guard rails: the abstraction boundary is in the wrong place."]

          [:h2 "The three-tier hybrid boundary"]

          [:p
           "Real-world algebraic-effect systems converge on a clear boundary. "
           [:a {:href "https://www.unison-lang.org/docs/language-reference/built-in-types/"} "Unison"]
           " keeps fixed-width "
           [:code "Nat"]
           ", "
           [:code "Int"]
           ", "
           [:code "Float"]
           ", "
           [:code "Text"]
           ", and "
           [:code "Bytes"]
           " as pure runtime builtins, handles "
           [:code "Abort"]
           ", "
           [:code "Exception"]
           ", "
           [:code "Store"]
           ", and "
           [:code "Stream"]
           " as library-level abilities, and reserves only "
           [:code "IO"]
           " for the runtime. "
           [:a {:href "https://koka-lang.github.io/koka/doc/book.html"} "Koka"]
           " keeps total math and data primitives completely effect-free ("
           [:code "total"]
           ") and starts effects at partiality ("
           [:code "exn"]
           "), divergence ("
           [:code "div"]
           "), state ("
           [:code "st"]
           "), and I/O. "
           [:a {:href "https://ocaml.org/manual/5.3/effects.html"} "OCaml 5"]
           " keeps hot synchronous primitives native, uses effect handlers for resumable non-local control flow, and explicitly forbids effects from crossing raw C callback frames."]

          [:p
           "Revision 6 of "
           [:code "yang.preludes.md"]
           " adopts that exact three-tier split for Yin/Yang:"]

          [:table.bench-results
           [:thead
            [:tr
             [:th "Tier"]
             [:th "Mechanism"]
             [:th "What lives here"]
             [:th "Why"]]]
           [:tbody
            [:tr
             [:td [:strong "1. Pure host value core"]]
             [:td [:code ":pure"] " host modules (" [:code "data"] ", " [:code "integer"] ", " [:code "float"] ", " [:code "kind"] ")"]
             [:td "Exact big-int math, boxed IEEE-754 float ops and formatting, " [:code "data/float64?"] ", " [:code "data/equiv"] ", " [:code "data/admit-key?"] ", " [:code "data/canonical-compare"] ", " [:code "data/canonical-entries"] ", " [:code "kind/namespace"]]
             [:td "One mathematical truth across all four hosts; native speed in tight loops; zero raw host doubles ever enter guest frames."]]
            [:tr
             [:td [:strong "2. Guest algebraic effects"]]
             [:td [:code "yrt.effect"] " over " [:code "%capture"] " + task-local handler stack"]
             [:td "Exceptions (" [:code "raise"] "/" [:code "try"] "/" [:code "finally"] "), escapes (" [:code "call-ec"] "), PHP " [:code "abandon-to"] ", resumable generators (" [:code "yield"] "), cold error/limit policy traps (" [:code ":math/div-zero"] ", " [:code ":math/overflow"] ", " [:code ":int/bit-limit"] "), and safepoint interrupts"]
             [:td "100% guest UAST (zero Jolt kernel cost); hot math pays zero dispatch overhead; one composable mechanism replaces four runtime subsystems."]]
            [:tr
             [:td [:strong "3. Minimal VM effects"]]
             [:td [:code "make-effect"] " to VM " [:code ":effect-handlers"]]
             [:td [:code "cell/*"] ", " [:code "stream/*"] " (" [:code "stream/poll!"] "), " [:code ":module/require"] ", and bound external I/O surfaces"]
             [:td "Preserves ADR 0004: external capabilities stay explicit stream deposits, never language-specific policy."]]]]

          [:p
           "The distinction between Tier 2 and Tier 3 is load-bearing. Putting language policy into VM host handlers would bloat Jolt's kernel and create a second effect medium beside "
           [:code "dao.stream"]
           ". Keeping algebraic effects in guest UAST over "
           [:code "%capture"]
           " gives every language swappable, composable handlers (Python "
           [:code "OverflowError"]
           " vs. JS "
           [:code "Infinity"]
           " vs. PHP int64-to-float demotion vs. deterministic test replay) without touching a single host engine."]

          [:p
           "And boxing IEEE-754 float operations at the "
           [:code "yin.vm.float"]
           " host boundary eliminates the raw-double hazard completely. Pure host kernels return a boxed value on the hot path and a pure status keyword on the cold path; a thin guest wrapper performs a "
           [:code "yrt.effect"]
           " trap only on that cold branch, with zero unwrapped host doubles anywhere on the stack."]

          [:h2 "Native plus N-to-1, not unnecessary N-by-M symmetry"]

          [:p
           "Earlier drafts also tried to enforce full N-by-M cross-frontend symmetry, including compiling "
           [:code "yang.clojure"]
           " syntax onto Python's mutable dictionaries via a generic hook table. That symmetry looks tidy on paper but taxes the compiler for combinations nobody needs."]

          [:p
           "The practical matrix is much cleaner:"]

          [:ul
           [:li
            [:strong "Native profiles (" [:code "py"] ", " [:code "js"] ", " [:code "php"] ", " [:code "clj"] "):"]
            " essential so existing programs written in Python, TypeScript, PHP, and Clojure execute with their real semantics."]
           [:li
            [:strong "N-to-1 Yin/Clojure profiles (" [:code ":yang.python/clojure-core-v1"] ", future " [:code ":yang.typescript/clojure-core-v1"] "):"]
            " the wolf-in-sheep's-clothing path for writing new datom.world code in familiar syntax directly on persistent data and "
            [:code "clojure.core"]
            "."]]

          [:p
           "Every profile, native or Clojure-backed, sits on the exact same Tier-1 pure host core and Tier-2 "
           [:code "yrt.effect"]
           " layer, and interoperates across language boundaries through "
           [:code "yang.call"]
           " with authenticated task-heap cells and private Layer-2b provenance doors."]

          [:h2 "Why this matters for datom.world"]

          [:p
           "Composable preludes keep the compiler and runtime small by deleting thousands of lines of duplicated guest machinery. Swappable N-to-1 preludes turn datom.world from a Lisp-only ecosystem into one any Python or TypeScript developer can write in on day one, while still running real persistent data structures, serializable continuations, and stream-native effects underneath."]

          [:p
           "One truth, many syntaxes, and the right abstraction at the right layer."]
          ]]]}
